        "titulo": "At the airport: fixed phrases and «in ritardo»",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: «il volo» is the flight, and a delayed flight is «in ritardo», like a train: «Il volo è in ritardo» (The flight is delayed). STEP 2: «la carta d'imbarco» is the boarding pass. In «d'imbarco» the «i» of «di» drops before a vowel. STEP 3: «l'imbarco» (boarding) is masculine, and its article becomes «l'» before a vowel. STEP 4: «il bagaglio a mano» is a fixed phrase and it keeps «a». English «luggage» cannot be counted, but «il bagaglio» can: «un bagaglio», «due bagagli». STEP 5: «la valigia» is the suitcase and «il passaporto» is the passport.",
        "comparacao": "English says «hand luggage» and «carry-on», and English joins the two words directly («hand luggage»), so there is no preposition to copy. Learners tend to reach for «di» and say «bagaglio di mano». Italian uses the fixed phrase «bagaglio a mano», and the «a» cannot be replaced. English also says «the flight is late», but the Italian for a delay is «in ritardo», as for trains.",
        "exemplos": [
          {
            "ru": "Il volo è in ritardo",
            "pt": "The flight is delayed"
          },
          {
            "ru": "il bagaglio a mano",
            "pt": "the hand luggage"
